<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : CONFIG_CHECK
      description : "Router bgp is configured"
      item        : "^router bgp"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CONFIG_CHECK
          description : "Verify a dedicated loopback interface must be configured and active"
          context     : "^\s*interface\s+loopback\d+"
          item        : "no\s+shutdown"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CONFIG_CHECK
          description : "Ensure all iBGP neighbor sessions use the stable loopback address as the source"
          context     : "^router bgp"
          context     : "neighbor\s+\d+"
          item        : "update-source\s+loopback\d+"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "OS10-RTR-000910 - The Dell OS10 BGP router must be configured to use its loopback address as the source address for iBGP peering sessions."
          info        : "Using a loopback address as the source address offers a multitude of uses for security, access, management, and scalability of the BGP routers. It is easier to construct appropriate ingress filters for router management plane traffic destined to the network management subnet since the source addresses will be from the range used for loopback interfaces instead of a larger range of addresses used for physical interfaces. Log information recorded by authentication and syslog servers will record the router's loopback address instead of the numerous physical interface addresses.

When the loopback address is used as the source for eBGP peering, the BGP session will be harder to hijack since the source address to be used is not known globally, making it more difficult for a hacker to spoof an eBGP neighbor. By using traceroute, a hacker can easily determine the addresses for an eBGP speaker when the IP address of an external interface is used as the source address. The routers within the iBGP domain should also use loopback addresses as the source address when establishing BGP sessions."
          solution    : "Ensure that the router's loopback address is used as the source address when originating traffic.

OS10(config)# router bgp 10
OS10(conf-router-bgp-10)# neighbor 192.0.2.4
OS10(conf-router-neighbor)# update-source Loopback 1"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,DISA_Benchmark|Dell_OS10_Switch_Router_STIG,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-269901r1052088_rule,STIG-ID|OS10-RTR-000910,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-269901"
          see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Dell_OS10_Switch_Y26M04_STIG.zip"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>
  </then>

  <else>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "OS10-RTR-000910 - The Dell OS10 BGP router must be configured to use its loopback address as the source address for iBGP peering sessions."
      info        : "Using a loopback address as the source address offers a multitude of uses for security, access, management, and scalability of the BGP routers. It is easier to construct appropriate ingress filters for router management plane traffic destined to the network management subnet since the source addresses will be from the range used for loopback interfaces instead of a larger range of addresses used for physical interfaces. Log information recorded by authentication and syslog servers will record the router's loopback address instead of the numerous physical interface addresses.

